// PENDING repro (NOT run by the pass harness) -- a NATIVE codegen miscompile in
// value machines: a value machine (one invoked as `self.x.method()` returning a
// value) that reads `self.<array field>[<index>]` in one of its arm/sub-states
// reads OFFSET 0 instead of the indexed byte -- UNLESS the index is a single
// literal token.
//
// Characterized 2026-07-08 (native x86_64, omega.exe):
//   value machine arm:   self.buf[5]        -> 99  (CORRECT: single literal)
//                        self.buf[5 + 0]    -> 0   (WRONG)
//                        self.buf[4 + 1]    -> 0   (WRONG)
//                        let j=5; self.buf[j] -> 0 (WRONG: local index)
//                        self.buf[self.i]   -> 0   (WRONG: field index)
//   PLAIN (statement) machine, same reads   -> 99  (all forms CORRECT)
//   interpreter, ALL forms                  -> 99  (masks the bug)
//
// So the miscompile is: in a VALUE machine, a NON-literal (local / field /
// const-arithmetic) index into a `self` array field resolves against the wrong
// base -- it reads the frame/zero base, not the receiver's array field. The
// single-literal path resolves the offset at compile time and is fine. This is
// almost certainly the "receiver threading still open" area
// ([[contained-machine-same-type-aliasing]]): the indexed-read base is not
// threaded with the value machine's receiver offset.
//
// BLOCKS the fs metadata per-target offset migration: data-driving the decode
// needs `stat_buf[ST_*_OFF + k]` (a const-arithmetic index) inside the
// `decode_metadata` VALUE machine, which this bug miscompiles to read offset 0.
// See [[value-machine-computed-index-miscompile]] and TASKS_FS.md metadata rung.
//
// This program exits 99 when correct, 0 when the bug is present. Run natively:
//   omega --build-dir <dir> main.omg && <dir>/omega-program.exe ; echo $?
boundary trait Console { machine exit_process(return_code: i32); }
data Probe { buf: [u8; 16]; }
machine Probe::fill(&mut self) -> i64 {
    self.buf[5] = 99;
    transition true { _ -> go() }
    state go(&mut self) -> i64 { let j: usize = 4 + 1; self.buf[j] as i64 }
}
data Main { console: Console; probe: Probe; out: i64; ncode: i32; }
machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    self.out = self.probe.fill();
    self.ncode = self.out as i32;
    self.console.exit_process(self.ncode);
}
